+
+### `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`
+
+Applies a [JSON Merge Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386) (RFC 7386) to a JSON document,
+and returns the result as a new value. `target` is never mutated.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+applyMergePatch({ foo: 'bar', baz: 'qux' }, { baz: null, extra: 1 }); // { foo: 'bar', extra: 1 }
+applyMergePatch({ foo: ['a', 'b'] }, { foo: ['c'] }); // { foo: ['c'] }
+```
+
+A merge patch that is itself a JSON object is merged into `target` member by member: a `null` member
+value removes the corresponding member from the result, any other value replaces it (recursively
+merging further if both sides are objects). A merge patch that is not an object — an array, string,
+number, boolean or `null` — replaces `target` entirely, since RFC 7386 only merges object members and
+treats every other JSON value as a wholesale replacement (so, unlike `applyPatch`, array members are
+always replaced as a whole, never merged element by element).
