+---
+id: dl-004-applymergepatch-immutability-and-prototype-safe-member-access
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch immutability and prototype-safe member access"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 7386's own reference algorithm (Appendix A) mutates `Target` in place and reads/writes its
+members with plain property access (`Target[Name]`). Neither matches this project's established
+contract for `document`/`target` parameters: `applyPatch` never mutates its input (`dl-002`), and
+plain property access on an object is unsafe for a member literally named `"__proto__"`, since
+`Object.prototype`'s inherited accessor intercepts both the read (when the own member is absent,
+returning `Object.prototype` instead of "no such member") and the write (reassigning the prototype
+instead of creating an own member) — the exact defect fixed for `applyPatch` in `dl-003`.
+
+## Decision
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` carries both of those precedents forward instead of following the
+RFC's pseudocode literally:
+
+1. **Immutability without a full deep clone.** The function never mutates `target`. Where the
+   pseudocode does `Target[Name] = MergePatch(Target[Name], Value)` in place, this implementation
+   starts from a shallow copy (`{ ...target }`) and only overwrites the members `patch` actually
+   touches, with the result of recursively merging into a *new* object at each level the recursion
+   reaches. Subtrees the patch does not touch are shared by reference between `target` and the
+   result — safe because nothing ever writes into them — so no `structuredClone` of the whole
+   document is needed, unlike `applyPatch`'s upfront clone (which has to guard a whole patch's worth
+   of in-place splicing, not a side-effect-free recursive rebuild).
+
+2. **Prototype-safe member access, both ways.** Writing a member reuses `applyPatch`'s `setMember`
+   helper (`Object.defineProperty`), not bracket assignment, so a patch member named `"__proto__"`
+   becomes a real own property. Reading a member for the recursive merge uses a new `getMember`
+   helper gated on `hasOwnProperty`, mirroring `resolveToken`'s existing read guard: without it,
+   merging a patch's `"__proto__"` member onto a target that has no own member of that name would
+   read `Object.prototype` itself (via the inherited accessor) as the "current value" to merge
+   into, rather than correctly treating the member as absent.
+
+No error semantics were added: RFC 7386, unlike RFC 6902, defines no failure cases, so
+`applyMergePatch` accepts any JSON value for `target` and `patch` and always returns a result.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can pass `applyMergePatch` a `target` they hold other references to; the result never
+  aliases or mutates it, but unpatched subtrees of the result may be the same object instances as in
+  `target` — mutating those later (outside this function) would be visible through the result too,
+  the same risk already accepted for `applyPatch`'s inserted values, which are also never deep-cloned.
+- Patch member names that collide with `Object.prototype` accessors (`"__proto__"`, and similarly
+  any inherited accessor name) behave as literal JSON member names in both directions: a `null` value
+  removes a real own member, a non-null value merges into whatever the real own member currently
+  holds (or nothing, if absent), never into the inherited accessor's value.
+- The `setMember`/`getMember` pair is now shared infrastructure for any future function in this
+  package that reads or writes an object member by a caller-supplied key; the precedent is to use
+  them instead of bracket access whenever the key is not a compile-time literal.
