@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-applymergepatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) applyMergePatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Some clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format (RFC 7386) rather than JSON
+Patch. Implement it: export `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`, applying a merge
+patch to a target JSON document and returning the merged document.
+
+## What was done
+
+Implemented `applyMergePatch` in `src/index.ts` directly from RFC 7386 §2 and the worked algorithm in
+Appendix A:
+
+- If `patch` is not a JSON object (array, string, number, boolean or `null`), it replaces `target`
+  entirely — returned as-is, same as `applyPatch`'s `add`/`replace` already do for inserted values
+  (no cloning).
+- If `patch` is a JSON object, `target` is first treated as an object too: if it already is one, its
+  own members become the starting point; otherwise (`target` is an array, a scalar, or `null`) the
+  starting point is an empty object, per RFC 7386's "ignore the contents and set it to an empty
+  Object." Each member of `patch` is then applied: a `null` value removes the member from the result;
+  any other value is merged in recursively (`applyMergePatch(currentValue, patchValue)`) and written
+  back.
+
+Two choices followed the precedent already set for `applyPatch` (see `dl-002`/`dl-003`) rather than
+re-deciding them from scratch:
+
+- **Immutability.** `target` is never mutated. Unlike the RFC's pseudocode (which mutates `Target` in
+  place), the starting point is a shallow copy (`{ ...target }`), and every member the patch actually
+  touches is overwritten with a freshly recursively-merged value rather than written into a shared
+  nested object. Untouched subtrees are shared by reference between `target` and the result (never
+  written to), so no deep clone is needed.
+- **Prototype-safe member access.** Writing a member uses the existing `setMember` helper
+  (`Object.defineProperty`), not bracket assignment, so a patch member named `"__proto__"` becomes a
+  real own property instead of reassigning the result's prototype. Reading a member for the recursive
+  merge uses a new `getMember` helper, gated on `hasOwnProperty` like `resolveToken` already is, so
+  that reading a not-yet-present `"__proto__"` member does not fall through to the inherited accessor
+  and return `Object.prototype` instead of "absent."
+
+RFC 7386 defines no error conditions (unlike RFC 6902), so `applyMergePatch` never throws; every JSON
+value is a valid `target` or `patch`.
+
+Added `test/merge-patch.test.ts`: every example from RFC 7386 Appendix A (replace, add, null-removal
+with one and several members, scalar-replaces-array and array-replaces-scalar, recursive object merge,
+array-replaces-array-of-objects wholesale, root replaced by an array/`null`/scalar, members surviving
+next to a `null`-valued one, a patch merged onto a non-object target, nested `null` removal), plus a
+non-mutation check and a `"__proto__"` member regression test (object literal syntax does not create
+an own property named `"__proto__"`, so the test builds the patch with `Object.defineProperty`, same
+technique already used in `test/apply-patch.test.ts`).
+
+Updated `README.md` with an `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` section matching the style of the
+existing `resolvePointer`/`applyPatch` sections.
+
+Confirmed the full suite (54 tests) and `tsc --noEmit` are clean.
+
+See `dl-004` for the design decisions behind immutability and prototype-safe member access.
